  backfill over [38;5;153mevents[39m.

  [1m[38;5;153mparticipations.reason[0m is [38;5;153mcreated | assigned | commented | manual[39m, upserted whenever you act on a task. [38;5;153mmanual[39m exists
  so an explicit watch button is a two-line addition, not a redesign.

  Three rules that are easy to get wrong and belong in the spec explicitly:

  1. [1mNever notify the actor of their own action.[0m The single most common notification bug. Fan-out excludes
     [38;5;153mevent.actor_id[39m.
  2. [1mFan-out happens in the same transaction as the event insert.[0m Otherwise a crash between them silently drops
     notifications, and there's no way to detect the gap afterward.
  3. [1mMute is sticky.[0m If you mute a task and later comment on it, you stay muted — the participation row is upserted but
     [38;5;153mmuted[39m is never reset by implicit activity. Only an explicit unmute clears it. The alternative (commenting implies
     re-interest) is defensible, but silent un-muting is the more annoying failure.

  Two things I deliberately left out. There's [1mno [38;5;153mdelivered_channels[39m column[0m, even though we're planning for email:
  retries, bounces, and per-channel timestamps need their own [38;5;153mnotification_deliveries[39m table, and a JSON column now would
  just be thrown away. The [38;5;153mnotifications[39m row is already the seam — that's enough. And there's [1mno preferences table[0m yet;
  with only three notifiable event types, per-type toggles are premature. Mute per task is the escape hatch that
  matters.

  [2m▎[0m [1;3mAssumption:[0;3m three notifiable event types is a low enough volume that users won't demand per-type preferences before
[0m  [2m▎[0m [3mwe ship. Validate by using the in-app inbox for a week before building preferences.[0m
